  "diagnosis_text": "The h1\u2192h5 ping fails. I found the s3\u2013s1 link disabled at both ends, while s3\u2019s rule for h5 still forwards to that disabled link. I\u2019ll check local connectivity at each host\u2019s switch to confirm the fault is in the inter-switch path rather than either host attachment.\n**The fault is localised to the s3\u2013s1 link: `s3-eth1` (port 1) \u2194 `s1-eth2` (port 2).** Forwarding still sends h5-bound traffic onto this disabled link.\n\nEvidence:\n- **h1 \u2192 h5:** 3/3 pings lost.\n- ONOS reports **both link endpoints disabled**, and the link is absent from the active topology.\n- h1 attaches to **s3 port 3**. On s3, the installed priority-41000 rule for h5\u2019s MAC (`00:00:00:00:00:05`) outputs to **port 1**, the disabled port. Its packet counter is nonzero.\n- **h1 \u2192 h2** and **h5 \u2192 h6:** both passed 3/3, supporting working local connectivity at both ends.\n\n**Diagnosis:** A down/disabled inter-switch link, combined with forwarding rules that have not rerouted around it, explains the outage. Alternate links through s2 are reported active, but the h5 rule on s3 does not use them.\n\n**Next step:** Inspect why `s3-eth1`/`s1-eth2` went down. If the outage is intentional, update the forwarding configuration to use an active path, including return traffic and ARP. These measurements do not establish what caused the link to be disabled; I have made no changes.",
